#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294091 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bfeec4f-a652-5945-80c5-e6faa1f8e3af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Inequality by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68959728-df6d-5aa8-813d-2349b4a9960d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294093} {:answer/id #uuid "1b580835-b73c-5ada-bcd2-267099de927c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [0,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294094} {:answer/id #uuid "1c5a976c-7776-56fa-88d0-fb30210ac2d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294095} {:answer/id #uuid "37032f17-d569-512e-bbd4-d80a588481f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup (4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294096} {:answer/id #uuid "2d780ba5-14b2-5e9f-8d6c-77d902c2f74e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [0,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d780ba5-14b2-5e9f-8d6c-77d902c2f74e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [0,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294097} :answer-field/id #uuid "67d5cdc8-0080-50bd-a2b3-565c9bbe5455" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04188be1-dec4-549b-9e2d-e556e183375a" :question/math-academy-id "q-115979" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{3}+x}{4-x}\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}+x}{4-x}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its numerator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x({x}^{2}+1)}{4-x}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solution to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=0.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=4.$ The root and asymptote divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,0),\\,(0,4),\\,(4,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals at the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| ${x}^{2}+1$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $4-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the interval $(0,4).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the root of $f(x)$ is also a solution, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dedebdf-6f3d-5982-a29a-9985de7f949f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Rational Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31a394a4-3b3e-53ba-b609-8850195dd65b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294100} {:answer/id #uuid "a6cefdd0-7ef3-5ef1-b056-1579c453f187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6cefdd0-7ef3-5ef1-b056-1579c453f187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294101} :answer-field/id #uuid "b007b3c2-ada5-5054-b3f3-3f58c23ae314" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294099} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39892fbf-8380-5b0b-963c-3615780aaced" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39892fbf-8380-5b0b-963c-3615780aaced" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294103} :answer-field/id #uuid "70851315-cfec-51b6-8150-def7181e0ec8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294102} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43a0231e-16a0-5768-a630-7d0f91003665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43a0231e-16a0-5768-a630-7d0f91003665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294105} :answer-field/id #uuid "9716469c-c1da-55a5-89c6-4983a3f905e3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a1905e1-52c0-58f8-8390-d9d1a449a196" :question/math-academy-id "q-274430" :question/problem "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{(x-2)(x-1)}{x+3}>0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)⋃($\n{{field-3}}$,\\,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(x-2)(x-1)}{x+3}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=1$ and $x=2.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-3.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-3),\\,(-3,1),\\,(1,2),\\,(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,1)$ | $(1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+3$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-3,-1)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\nf(x)>0\\,\\text{for all}\\,x\\in (-3,1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bfeec4f-a652-5945-80c5-e6faa1f8e3af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Inequality by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e70649-c311-5b0f-ab2c-a9e78495fa07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01e70649-c311-5b0f-ab2c-a9e78495fa07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294108} :answer-field/id #uuid "92cb2bbb-1d54-513b-8367-a72dde7ffe8b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294107} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ffe037c-3d23-516f-b0ec-2041d731ccd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ffe037c-3d23-516f-b0ec-2041d731ccd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294110} :answer-field/id #uuid "02747366-9c88-5585-8e70-aa65238eaa87" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294109} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e339bbc2-03a5-5eec-b842-ea776add95b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e339bbc2-03a5-5eec-b842-ea776add95b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294112} :answer-field/id #uuid "8517fdf8-7377-58ce-a0cf-87c49aa5f2b0" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ba6880bf-2980-5277-ba16-e7a358e780ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-326141" :question/problem "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{2{x}^{2}-x-3}{x+5}\\ge 0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$]⋃[$\n{{field-3}}$,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-x-3}{x+5}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its numerator and denominator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(2x-3)(x+1)}{x+5}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=-1.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-5.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5),\\,(-5,-1),\\,(-1,\\frac{3}{2}),\\,(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,-1)$ | $(-1,\\frac{3}{2})$ | $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive  on the intervals $(-5,-1)$ and $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots of $f(x)$ are also solutions, we conclude that \n\n$$\nf(x)\\ge 0\\,\\text{for all}\\,x\\in (\\,-5,-1\\,]⋃[\\,\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dedebdf-6f3d-5982-a29a-9985de7f949f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Rational Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bfa8b92-1871-537d-9f3f-cfa616cf5aaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: x ∈ [2, ∞)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170858} {:answer/id #uuid "95649d24-1689-5c2d-b4a4-bf243bc46d09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: x ∈ (-5, 1]∪[2, ∞)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170859} {:answer/id #uuid "b506c03c-1f7e-5df6-a3b9-502f37f691c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (- ∞,-5)∪[1, 2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170860} {:answer/id #uuid "efddcb0b-bf14-59c9-9612-7d8b40be2fd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: x ∈ (- ∞,-5]∪[1, 2]]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170861} {:answer/id #uuid "747e26f3-f577-5e22-8eb2-9a4d08bb7dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: x ∈ [- 5, 1]∪[2, ∞)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95649d24-1689-5c2d-b4a4-bf243bc46d09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: x ∈ (-5, 1]∪[2, ∞)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170859} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb9cc95b-5287-53e8-8f3c-a80cc1b2a701" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e943f7b2-e03c-5781-a978-e2ace7becf14" :question/math-academy-id "q-38472" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{(1-x)(x-2)}{x+5}\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(1-x)(x-2)}{x+5}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=1$ and $x=2.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-5.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5),\\,(-5,1),\\,(1,2),\\,(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals at the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,1)$ | $(1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-5,1)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$ \n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots of $f(x)$ are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\le 0$ for all $x\\in (-5,1]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bfeec4f-a652-5945-80c5-e6faa1f8e3af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Inequality by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b509ad2d-bad6-546a-a6c0-443537ae02be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{3}{2},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294115} {:answer/id #uuid "6621059d-1005-5237-bbdf-ca6fdd3292bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{3}{2})\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294116} {:answer/id #uuid "8b2cbf36-1697-574d-845b-009a32c87ddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294117} {:answer/id #uuid "37d41976-f5af-55af-8cb2-0b4ecb7579ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294118} {:answer/id #uuid "73d3782d-2786-5186-be37-6762252d96f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{3}{2},1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37d41976-f5af-55af-8cb2-0b4ecb7579ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294118} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d674fd0-0e52-592a-9b8a-107b33e77808" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2eb3e674-f935-51dd-8bbd-d47ae74a23f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-88824" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{x-1}{4{x}^{2}+12x+9}>0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x-1}{4{x}^{2}+12x+9}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its denominator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x-1}{(2x+3)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=1.$  Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ The root and asymptote divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-\\frac{3}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{3}{2},1),\\,(1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{3}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{3}{2},1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(2x+3)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the interval $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$"}]}
